  "raw": {
    "https://txwes.peopleadmin.com/postings/6533": {
      "title": "Coordinator of First-Year Writing (Internal Hire)",
      "description": "Texas Wesleyan University Portal | Coordinator of First-Year Writing (Internal Hire) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Contact Us Coordinator of First-Year Writing (Internal Hire) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Job Title Coordinator of First-Year Writing (Internal Hire) Position Summary Incoming college students face an increasingly complex set of academic challenges, from widening gaps in post-secondary writing readiness to the rapid integration of emerging technologies like generative AI. Mandatory introductory composition courses—ENG 1301 and ENG 1302—serve as the foundation for undergraduate academic success, critical thinking, and institutional retention. To ensure instructional consistency, elevate course quality, and systematically track student learning outcomes, the director will provide dedicated pedagogical leadership, comprehensive programmatic assessment, and targeted faculty mentorship, serving as a vital bridge between first-year academic initiatives and institutional student success goals. The person in this position will be Director of First-Year Writing and teach 3 courses each semester. The Director of First-Year Writing will address key institutional priorities by: Bridging the Readiness Divide: Supporting incoming students as they transition from high school to rigorous, college-level critical thinking, research, and writing. Standardizing Core Curriculum Quality: Ensuring programmatic consistency and equitable learning standards across all sections of ENG 1301 and ENG 1302. Empowering Faculty: Providing robust onboarding, continuous pedagogical development, and instructional mentorship—especially for adjunct instructors. Driving Data-Informed Retention: Connecting writing assessment data directly to institutional persistence strategies and general education outcomes. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Teach 3 classes. Supporting incoming students as they transition from high school to rigorous, college-level critical thinking, research, and writing. Ensuring programmatic consistency and equitable learning standards across all sections of ENG 1301 and ENG 1302. Providing robust onboarding, continuous pedagogical development, and instructional mentorship—especially for adjunct instructors. Connecting writing assessment data directly to institutional persistence strategies and general education outcomes. Other duties as assigned by the chair of the department. A yearly report will be produced by the person in this position due in May. In addition, this person will be in charge of completing the GEC analysis. This is not a report like program review, but is much shorter to include the information needed for GEC . Education, Certifications and/or Licenses A Master’s or Ph.D. in English, preferably with a specialty in writing. Experience At least 5 years of college level instruction. Experience with college level assessment. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Microsoft Word. Data entry software. College level assessment. Posting Detail Information Posting Number 2016AS1132P Posting Open Date 09/14/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Posting Close Date Special Instructions to Applicants Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Optional Documents Resume Cover Letter Current Employee Application (for internal applicants) 1201 Wesleyan Street Fort Worth, TX 76105 | 817-531-4444 ©2016 Texas Wesleyan University. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://txwes.peopleadmin.com/postings/6389": {
      "title": "Dean of Education",
      "description": "Texas Wesleyan University Portal | Dean of Education Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Contact Us Dean of Education Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Job Title Dean of Education Position Summary The Dean of Education is charged with establishing, implementing, and evaluating the strategic priorities of the School of Education. The Dean reports directly to the Provost and Senior Vice President of Academic Affairs and provides leadership to the program directors, faculty, and staff in the School of Education. Working with the program directors, the Dean is responsible for the School’s academic program quality, assessment of curriculum and instruction, fostering faculty research and scholarship, budgeting and fiscal management, fundraising, and new program development. Successful candidates must possess a record of academic and scholarly excellence that meets or exceeds the University’s criteria for appointment as a full professor. The start date for this position is August 1, 2026 Essential Duties and Responsibilities Leadership & Strategic Direction Provide leadership and direction to instructional and program staff, faculty, and adjunct faculty in developing and sustaining programs, instructional strategies, and program offerings. Administer and maintain accurate records for students, faculty and support staff, such as advising reports, professional development plans, annual evaluations and other personnel records as appropriate for specific program needs and responsibilities of the school. Develop goals and objectives for programs and participate in implementing accountability systems to ensure alignment with policies and procedures. Provide input to the Provost on long-range and strategic planning activities. Recommend employment, retention, promotion, and termination of instructors and staff to the Provost. Assist in resolving faculty and staff issues, including student grievances, academic integrity, and safety concerns. Serve as chair for the steering committee of the Leadership Academy Network (1882 partnership) and provide guidance and oversight for the LAN Senior Director and staff. Curriculum & Program Oversight Lead the process of new program development and revision of existing programs, together with the faculty of the school in keeping with strategic planning initiatives and seek accreditation and approvals by relevant state, regional, national and professional accrediting agencies. Ensure assessment of student learning outcomes in accordance with University policies. Promote revenue growth through new initiatives, grants, and continuing education opportunities. Accreditation & Compliance Ensure timely completion of accreditation and program approval requirements. Coordinate and document required program approval and/or accreditation activities. Maintain active program advisory committees to guide School of Education programs. Operations & Resource Management Direct and oversee School operations, including budget planning, management, and faculty loads/contracts. Supervise the school budget, approve requisitions and travel requests, and submit annual budget requests to the Provost. In conjunction with program directors, plan and coordinate course schedules to meet student needs and enrollment targets, optimizing faculty loads, budgets, and space. Lead development of a data-informed, student-centered master schedule to support student completion and workforce alignment. Support Advancement fundraising activities and initiatives in addition to developing a compelling vision for the school, cultivating relationships with donors, and strategically aligning fundraising efforts with institutional goals. Communication & Representation Represent the School of Education to partners (ISDs, TEA , etc.), external agencies, and professional/community groups. Coordinate with Marketing and Communications to develop and implement a marketing strategy for the School’s programs. Communicate policies, standards, and expectations to faculty and staff, and hold employees accountable. Build and maintain positive working relationships with University colleagues and external partners. Actively serve on internal and external task forces, committees, and work groups. Attend internal and external events to represent the School of Education. These include but are not limited to graduation, honors ceremonies, open houses, orientation, meetings with leadership teams in school districts. Administrative Duties Collaborate in developing policies, practices, processes, and programs for the School. Participate in professional development appropriate to the position. Schedule and preside over School meetings and maintain meeting minutes. Monitor instruction, conduct performance appraisals, and assist with professional development planning. Research and prepare technical and administrative reports and written correspondence. Work in conjunction with the Vice President for Finance, Vice President for Enrollment and other university departments in developing and monitoring initiatives for student enrollment and retention, including budget preparation. Other duties as assigned. Education, Certifications and/or Licenses Dean must have earned academic doctorate, and appropriate professional and clinical credentials in a discipline represented in or related to the School of Education. Experience Requires a minimum of five years of full-time experience within Texas public schools and higher education institutions. Higher education experience is expected. A record of successful administrative leadership at the dean, chair, or program director level, including budget and personnel experience or other related administrative experience Experience with professionally accredited academic programs and familiarity with compliance and risk management practices. Experience with clinical site development and oversight including creating partnerships integral to student field experiences A data informed planner with experience in assessment and outcomes measurements. A record of advanced learning, innovation, or teaching experience incorporating both traditional and distance education programs Experience working with external agencies, such as foundations, local businesses, and governmental agencies Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Knowledge, skills, and abilities commensurate with key responsibilities. Knowledge of the Texas Education Agency requirements for teacher education programs and experience with curricular design or redesign to meet these requirements. Posting Detail Information Posting Number 2016AS1099P Posting Open Date 04/20/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Posting Close Date Special Instructions to Applicants Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is the highest level of education attained? GED High School Diploma Associates Degree Bachelors Degree Masters Degree PHD * Will you have a Ph.D. in hand by the date of appointment or an advanced ABD by the date of the appointment? Yes No Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Transcripts Letter of Recommendation Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Current Employee Application (for internal applicants) List of Three References 1201 Wesleyan Street Fort Worth, TX 76105 | 817-531-4444 ©2016 Texas Wesleyan University. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://txwes.peopleadmin.com/postings/6549": {
      "title": "Dean of the School of Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Texas Wesleyan University Portal | Dean of the School of Arts and Sciences Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Contact Us Dean of the School of Arts and Sciences Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Anticipated start date of Summer 2027. Posting Details Position Information Job Title Dean of the School of Arts and Sciences Position Summary Serves as Chief Academic Officer for the School of Arts and Sciences, provides advocacy for, leadership to and has managerial responsibility for the programs, budgets, course schedules, faculty and students of the School. Serves as a representative of the University administration to the faculty, staff and students of the School, as well as to the community. Reports directly to the Provost and Senior Vice President. Working with the department chairs and program directors, the Dean is responsible for the School’s academic program quality, assessment of curriculum and instruction, fostering faculty research and scholarship, budgeting and fiscal management, fundraising, and new program development. Teaches courses in their discipline as appropriate to the needs of the School and in consultation with the Provost/Senior Vice President. Serves on appropriate University committees, task forces, search committees and in other roles as requested. Anticipated start date of Summer 2027 Essential Duties and Responsibilities Leadership and Strategic Direction Provide leadership in support of the University’s strategic plan as well as in the development and execution of the School’s strategic plan, goals, and assessment. Provide leadership and direction to instructional and program staff, faculty, and adjunct faculty in developing and sustaining programs, instructional strategies, and program offerings. Recruit, develop and retain quality faculty and staff members. Evaluate faculty and staff based on performance criteria that supports and advances the School and the University. Recommend employment, retention, promotion, and termination of instructors and staff to the Provost. Assist in resolving faculty and staff issues, including student grievances, academic integrity, and safety concerns. Curriculum and Program Oversight Oversee the School’s academic programs to ensure quality and relevance. Ensure that effective program planning and assessment is carried out in the School to accomplish the goals and objectives of the School’s and University’s Strategic Plan as well as to meet accreditation standards of SACSCOC . Lead the process of new program development and revision of existing programs, together with the faculty of the School in keeping with strategic planning initiatives and seek accreditation and approvals by relevant state, regional, national and professional accrediting agencies. Ensure assessment of student learning outcomes in accordance with University policies. Promote revenue growth through new initiatives, grants, and continuing education opportunities. Accreditation and Compliance Ensure timely completion of accreditation and program approval requirements. Coordinate and document required program approval and/or accreditation activities. Maintain active program advisory committees to guide School programs. Operations and Resource Management Manage course schedule and faculty load to positively impact budget through increasing average class sizes; reducing adjunct and stipend costs; and increasing revenue and contribution to the University. Develop and manage operating and restricted budgets within the School utilizing the financial and material resources of the School effectively and efficiently. Supervise the School budget, approve requisitions and travel requests, and submit annual budget requests to the Provost. In conjunction with department chairs and program directors, plan and coordinate course schedules to meet student needs and enrollment targets, optimizing faculty loads, budgets, and space. Conduct fundraising cultivation and solicitation in coordination with the Office of Advancement to raise funding for capital projects, new technology, scholarships, chairs, professorships, University endowment, and grants from federal, foundation, and/or corporate sources. Communication and Representation Represent the School’s and University’s mission, vision, and values to multiple constituencies including but not limited to the Board, community at large, area business community, the media, current and prospective students, and alumni. Develop and maintain strong relationships with counterparts in all area community colleges, School alumni, and stakeholders and constituents in the community; and speak in the community on behalf of the School and the University. Coordinate with Marketing and Communications to develop and implement a marketing strategy for the School’s programs. Communicate policies, standards, and expectations to faculty and staff, and hold employees accountable. Build and maintain positive working relationships with University colleagues and external partners. Actively serve on internal and external task forces, committees, and work groups. Attend internal and external events to represent the School. These include but are not limited to graduation, honors ceremonies, open houses, and orientation. Administrative Duties Collaborate in developing policies, practices, processes, and programs for the School. Participate in professional development appropriate to the position. Schedule and preside over School meetings and maintain meeting minutes. Administer and maintain accurate records for students, faculty and support staff, such as advising reports, professional development plans, annual evaluations and other personnel records as appropriate for specific program needs and responsibilities of the School. Research and prepare technical and administrative reports and written correspondence. Supervise and/or participate in student recruitment, advisement and retention efforts. Work in conjunction with the Vice President for Finance, Vice President for Enrollment and other University departments in developing and monitoring initiatives for student enrollment and retention, including budget preparation. Other duties as assigned by the Provost and Senior Vice President. Supervisory Responsibilities Carries out supervisory responsibilities in accordance with the University’s policies and applicable laws. Responsibilities include interviewing, hiring, and training employees; planning, assigning, and directing work; appraising performance; rewarding and disciplining employees; and addressing complaints and resolving problems. Education, Certifications and/or Licenses Dean must have an earned doctorate in an Arts or Science-related discipline. Experience A record of significant experience in higher education administration is preferred. Must have teaching experience in a discipline appropriate to the School. Experience with accreditation assessment processes required. A record of successful administrative leadership at the dean, chair, or program director level, including budget and personnel experience or other related administrative experience. Experience with professionally accredited academic programs and familiarity with compliance and risk management practices. A data-informed planner with experience in assessment and outcomes measurements. A record of advanced learning, innovation, or teaching experience incorporating both traditional and distance education programs. Experience working with external agencies, such as foundations, local businesses, and governmental agencies. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Knowledge of School and University curriculum and major issues in higher education, including tenure, faculty/staff development, program evaluation, and accreditation. Must have instructional specialty appropriate to the School. Outstanding organizational abilities with strong detail orientation. Ability to work on multiple tasks and meet project deadlines. Ability to work individually, as well as in a team-based environment. Familiarity with higher education budgeting and scheduling processes. Clear understanding of assessment processes and the ability to conduct needs studies in an institutional framework. An understanding of accreditation issues. Knowledge of rules, standards, regulations, and laws regarding student records. Ability to investigate, analyze data and draw conclusions. Ability to be discreet in handling confidential matters, while complying with FERPA regulations. Proficiency with an integrated administrative system and current computer software programs. Knowledge of human resources concepts, practices, policies, and procedures. Ability to adhere to University and departmental policies and procedures. Ability to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community. Ability to make administrative/procedural decisions and judgments. Ability to supervise and evaluate employees. Ability to maintain a professional appearance and office atmosphere. Ability to develop, plan, implement, and evaluate programs and short- and long-range goals. Skill in organizing resources and establishing priorities. Skill in budget preparation and fiscal management. Skill in course scheduling and institutional and unit planning. Outstanding interpersonal and community-relations skills. Posting Detail Information Posting Number 2016AS1139P Posting Open Date 10/26/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Posting Close Date Special Instructions to Applicants Anticipated start date of Summer 2027. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Transcripts Letter of Recommendation Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Resume Current Employee Application (for internal applicants) List of Three References 1201 Wesleyan Street Fort Worth, TX 76105 | 817-531-4444 ©2016 Texas Wesleyan University. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://txwes.peopleadmin.com/postings/6548": {
      "title": "Visiting Professor - Undergraduate Education - School of Education",
      "description": "Texas Wesleyan University Portal | Visiting Professor - Undergraduate Education - School of Education Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Contact Us Visiting Professor - Undergraduate Education - School of Education Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Job Title Visiting Professor - Undergraduate Education - School of Education Position Summary The Texas Wesleyan University School of Education invites applications for a Visiting Professor of Undergraduate Education to teach courses that support the preparation of future EC–6 educators, including graduate courses emphasizing early literacy, writing, and English as a Second Language ( ESL ) . This position includes the possibility of conversion to a tenure-track position. The successful candidate may teach in the graduate programs, including master’s and doctoral classes. The candidate will bring strong knowledge of effective teaching and learning practices in early childhood and elementary education and demonstrated commitment to preparing highly effective, culturally responsive educators. The Visiting Professor will contribute to the School of Education’s educator preparation mission through high-quality instruction, student mentoring, and collaboration with faculty and school partners. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Teach undergraduate courses in early literacy, reading, writing, ESL , and related areas of elementary education . Prepare and deliver engaging, rigorous, and student-centered instruction aligned with the needs of EC–6 teacher candidates. Integrate evidence-based literacy practices and effective instructional strategies into coursework and clinical preparation. Prepare teacher candidates to support diverse learners, including multilingual learners/English learners, in elementary classrooms. Incorporate appropriate connections to the Science of Teaching Reading ( STR ) and current research-based literacy practices. Support teacher candidates in developing knowledge and skills related to reading, writing, language development, assessment, and differentiated instruction. Connect coursework to authentic classroom practices and the expectations of contemporary EC–6 schools. Advise, mentor, and support undergraduate teacher candidates as appropriate. Collaborate with School of Education faculty, university colleagues, and school/community partners. Participate in faculty meetings, program activities, assessment, and other responsibilities associated with the University’s educator preparation programs. Contribute to the continuous improvement of the School of Education’s undergraduate educator preparation programs. Other duties as assigned. Education, Certifications and/or Licenses An earned doctorate in Education, Curriculum and Instruction, Literacy, Reading, TESOL / ESL , Elementary Education, or a closely related field is preferred. Candidates who are ABD (All But Dissertation) or are actively pursuing a doctoral degree may also be considered, provided they demonstrate satisfactory progress toward degree completion and meet the other qualifications for the position. A minimum of three years of successful teaching experience in EC–6 grades . Demonstrated knowledge of early literacy and writing instruction . Demonstrated knowledge and experience related to ESL /English learner education . Experience preparing, mentoring, or supporting teacher candidates is highly desirable. Strong communication, interpersonal, and organizational skills. Commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching and educator preparation. Texas Teacher Certificate. Experience Experience teaching at the university level. Texas EC–6 teaching certification or equivalent elementary teaching credential. Experience with teacher preparation and clinical/field-based experiences. Knowledge of the Science of Teaching Reading ( STR ) and evidence-based literacy instruction. Experience working with multilingual learners/English learners in elementary settings. Knowledge of Texas educator standards, EC–6 certification requirements, and/or relevant state standards. Experience integrating culturally responsive and inclusive instructional practices into teacher preparation. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities The successful candidate will demonstrate: Strong content knowledge in early literacy, writing, and ESL . Ability to translate research and theory into practical classroom applications. Ability to prepare teacher candidates to meet the diverse academic and linguistic needs of EC–6 learners. Commitment to student success and mentoring. Ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, school partners, and community stakeholders. Commitment to continuous improvement in educator preparation. Posting Detail Information Posting Number 2016AS1137P Posting Open Date 10/12/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Posting Close Date Special Instructions to Applicants Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Transcripts Curriculum Vitae Education Certification (if necessary) List of Three References Optional Documents Current Employee Application (for internal applicants) Letter of Recommendation 1201 Wesleyan Street Fort Worth, TX 76105 | 817-531-4444 ©2016 Texas Wesleyan University. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    }
